Operator-pasted terminal transcript — D-10 eyes-on re-run, 2026-08-17
=====================================================================

PROVENANCE / STATUS OF THIS ARTIFACT
------------------------------------
This is an OBSERVATIONAL re-run, captured for D-10's eyes-on half only. It is NOT
Gate 3's recorded machine measurement — that remains plan 145-07's run, whose
capture is logs/pulse4688.stderr.raw and whose extracted counts are
logs/frames_pulse4688.txt. Nothing in this file supersedes or re-measures Gate 3.

Why this run happened: the 145-07 Gate 3 run executed inside a background agent, so
the operator had no live terminal to watch. The operator was offered (a) recording
the eyes-on half as NOT COLLECTED or (b) a foreground re-run, and selected the
re-run. They ran the command themselves in their own terminal — the orchestrator did
not run it — because tool-issued command output is not reliably rendered live in the
operator's UI, and spending a program cycle on an observation they still could not
make would have been wasteful.

Command, exactly as the operator ran it from /workspaces:

    firestarter -v -p /dev/ttyACM0 write W27C512 .planning/phases/145-bench-validation/images/img_4k_pulse.bin --pulse-us 4688

Cost: one further erase-and-program cycle on the seated W27C512, reprogramming the
same image the part already held. Authorized by the operator's selection.

OPERATOR'S VERBATIM WORDS
-------------------------
The operator's complete free-text description of what they saw, quoted exactly as
typed, not cleaned up, not corrected, not paraphrased:

    It looked ok

That is the entirety of their prose description. They then pasted the transcript
reproduced below. Per D-10, the eyes-on half is recorded as the operator gave it —
this record does not embellish four words into a narrative, and does not attribute
to the operator any observation they did not make. The operator did NOT state a
per-block movement count, did NOT describe the bar's motion as smooth or stepped,
and did NOT comment on the terminal bar percentage; any such reading below is
derived by the orchestrator FROM THE PASTED TEXT, and is labelled as such.

